12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the working principle and construction of Semiconductor Injection Laser Diodes \(GaAs\). Discuss why direct bandgap materials are mandatory for lasing.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Direct vs. Indirect Bandgap Semiconductors:) Tj
T*
(   - In a Direct Bandgap material \(e.g., Gallium Arsenide, GaAs; InP\), the conduction band minimum) Tj
T*
(   and valence band maximum align at the identical crystal wavevector \(k = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - An electron can recombine directly with a hole, conserving crystal momentum without requiring) Tj
T*
(   phonon interaction: Rate of radiative recombination is high \(~ 10^-9 s\).) Tj
T*
(   - In an Indirect Bandgap material \(e.g., Silicon, Germanium\), the band extrema occur at) Tj
T*
(   different k values. Recombination requires simultaneous emission or absorption of a crystal) Tj
T*
(   lattice vibration \(phonon\). Non-radiative processes dominate; stimulated emission cannot) Tj
T*
(   overcome internal absorption.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, direct bandgap semiconductors are mandatory for laser diodes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Construction of GaAs Laser Diode:) Tj
T*
(   - Heavily doped \(degenerate\) p-type and n-type GaAs forming a p-n junction.) Tj
T*
(   - Fermi levels lie within the conduction band \(n-side\) and valence band \(p-side\).) Tj
T*
(   - A narrow active recombination zone \(~ 0.1 to 0.2 microns thick\) exists at the planar) Tj
T*
(   interface.) Tj
T*
(   - Opposite cleavage facets of the semiconductor crystal \(along [110] planes\) are naturally flat) Tj
T*
(   and parallel, serving as Fabry-Perot resonator mirrors due to Fresnel reflection \(reflectivity) Tj
T*
(   R ~= \(n-1\)^2 / \(n+1\)^2 ~ 32% for GaAs with n = 3.6\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Population Inversion via Forward Bias:) Tj
T*
(   - A strong forward bias voltage V > E_g / e is applied across the junction.) Tj
T*
(   - Massive injection of electrons from n-side and holes from p-side floods the junction) Tj
T*
(   transition region.) Tj
T*
(   - This creates an inverted population where electrons in the conduction band outnumber) Tj
T*
(   electrons in the valence band over an energy interval: E_Fn - E_Fp > h*nu > E_g) Tj
T*
(   \(Bernard-Duraffourg condition\).) Tj
T*
(   - Stimulated electron-hole recombination emits coherent photons at lambda = h*c / E_g ~=) Tj
T*
(   850-904 nm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the threshold condition for lasing in an optical resonant cavity. Obtain the mathematical expression for the threshold gain coefficient gamma_th.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Resonant Cavity Model:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider an active laser medium of length L placed between two mirrors of power) Tj
T*
(   reflectivities R1 and R2.) Tj
T*
(   - Let the small-signal gain coefficient of the active medium be gamma \(per unit length\).) Tj
T*
(   - Let internal distributed losses \(scattering, diffraction, free-carrier absorption\) be) Tj
T*
(   characterized by loss coefficient alpha \(per unit length\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Round-Trip Power Evolution:) Tj
T*
(   - Trace an initial optical beam of intensity I_0 starting at mirror 1 \(z = 0\) traveling toward) Tj
T*
(   mirror 2:) Tj
T*
(   * Traversal from z = 0 to z = L: Intensity amplified by factor exp\(\(gamma - alpha\) * L\).) Tj
T*
(   * Reflection at mirror 2: Reflected intensity = R2 * I_0 * exp\(\(gamma - alpha\) * L\).) Tj
T*
(   * Return traversal from z = L back to z = 0: Amplified again by exp\(\(gamma - alpha\) * L\).) Tj
T*
(   * Reflection at mirror 1: Final intensity after one complete round trip:) Tj
T*
(   I_round_trip = R1 * R2 * I_0 * exp\( 2 * \(gamma - alpha\) * L \).) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
