ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Mode Locking in lasers. How does it produce ultrashort femtosecond optical pulses, and what are its potential civil material testing applications?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept of Mode Locking:) Tj
T*
(   - A laser cavity supports numerous longitudinal modes separated by frequency Delta_nu = c /) Tj
T*
(   \(2*L\).) Tj
T*
(   - In an ordinary multi-mode laser, these modes oscillate with random, uncorrelated phases,) Tj
T*
(   resulting in an average continuous output with random fluctuations.) Tj
T*
(   - In Mode Locking, a fixed phase relationship \(Delta_phi = constant\) is forced across all) Tj
T*
(   oscillating modes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Description of Pulse Formation:) Tj
T*
(   - The total electric field is the coherent superposition of 2N+1 locked modes:) Tj
T*
(   E\(t\) = Sum_{n=-N}^N E_0 * exp[ j * \(\(omega_0 + n*Delta_omega\)*t + n*phi\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Summing the geometric series yields:) Tj
T*
(   E\(t\) = E_0 * exp\(j*omega_0*t\) * [ sin\(\(2N+1\)*Delta_omega*t / 2\) / sin\(Delta_omega*t / 2\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - The resulting intensity consists of a periodic train of intense ultrashort pulses with:) Tj
T*
(   * Pulse repetition period: T = 2 * L / c \(cavity round-trip time\).) Tj
T*
(   * Pulse duration: tau_pulse ~= 1 / \(Total Gain Bandwidth\) = 1 / \(\(2N+1\) * Delta_nu\).) Tj
T*
(   * Peak power: P_peak = \(2N+1\)^2 * P_0 \(amplified by square of mode count\).) Tj
T*
(   - Titanium:Sapphire lasers achieve pulse durations down to 10 to 100 femtoseconds \(10^-15 s\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Civil Materials Applications:) Tj
T*
(   - Femtosecond Laser Micromachining: Inscription of Fiber Bragg Gratings \(FBG\) into optical) Tj
T*
(   fiber sensors for dam and bridge monitoring without thermal damage to silica cladding.) Tj
T*
(   - Laser Induced Breakdown Spectroscopy \(LIBS\): Ultra-fast elemental depth profiling of chloride) Tj
T*
(   and carbonation penetration in marine concrete infrastructure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A laser distance meter measures distance across an expansion span. If the atmospheric temperature increases from 15 C to 35 C at standard pressure \(1013.25 hPa\), determine the fractional error in measured distance if no meteorological correction is applied.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Atmospheric Refraction and Group Index:) Tj
T*
(   - The velocity of light in air is v = c / n_g, where n_g is the group refractive index of air.) Tj
T*
(   - Simplified Edlen formula for air refractive index as a function of temperature T \(in Kelvin\)) Tj
T*
(   and pressure P \(in hPa\):) Tj
T*
(   \(n_g - 1\) * 10^6 ~= 287.6 * \(P / 1013.25\) * [ 273.15 / T ].) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Calculation at Initial State \(T1 = 15 C = 288.15 K, P = 1013.25 hPa\):) Tj
T*
(   - \(n1 - 1\) * 10^6 = 287.6 * 1.0 * \(273.15 / 288.15\) = 287.6 * 0.9479 = 272.6 ppm.) Tj
T*
(   - n1 = 1.0002726.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
